.TH CYVIEW 1 "December 2025" "cyview" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cyview \- view Cytoscape.js JSON graphs (.cyjs) with a lightweight local web viewer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cyview
.RI [ options ] " FILE.cyjs"
.PP
.B cyview
.RI [ options ] " \-"
.PP
.I producer
| \fBcyview\fR [options]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcyview\fR is a small Linux command that opens an interactive viewer for Cytoscape.js JSON graphs (commonly stored with the \fI.cyjs\fR extension).
It runs a tiny local HTTP server and opens a browser window (preferably Chromium in app mode) to display the diagram.

.SH INPUT FORMAT
\fBcyview\fR expects CYJS content: JSON compatible with Cytoscape.js element definitions.
At minimum, the document should contain an \fBelements\fR object with \fBnodes\fR and \fBedges\fR:

.nf
{
  "elements": {
    "nodes": [
      { "data": { "id": "a", "label": "A" } },
      { "data": { "id": "b", "label": "B" } }
    ],
    "edges": [
      { "data": { "id": "ab", "source": "a", "target": "b", "label": "depends" } }
    ]
  }
}
.fi

Compound (nested) nodes are supported via the \fBparent\fR field in a node's \fBdata\fR.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-ui " {auto|chromium|xdg-open|print-url}"
Select how the viewer is opened.
\fBauto\fR prefers Chromium app mode if available, otherwise falls back to \fBxdg-open\fR.
\fBchromium\fR forces Chromium app mode.
\fBxdg-open\fR forces default browser via \fBxdg-open\fR.
\fBprint-url\fR does not open a browser; it prints the viewer URL and continues serving until interrupted (or until \fB--once\fR triggers).

.TP
.BR \-\-styles " NAME"
Select a style scheme. The scheme controls which \fBcyview.styles\fR file name is searched and applied.
If not specified, \fBcyview\fR uses the environment variable \fBCYVIEW_STYLES\fR if set; otherwise it uses \fBdefault\fR.

.TP
.BR \-\-bind " ADDR"
Bind address for the local HTTP server. Default is \fB127.0.0.1\fR.
If you use \fB0.0.0.0\fR, the server binds all interfaces (be careful).

.TP
.BR \-\-port " PORT"
Port for the local HTTP server. Default is \fB0\fR (choose a free ephemeral port).

.TP
.BR \-\-once
Exit after the viewer page (\fIviewer.html\fR) is served the first time (best-effort).

.TP
.BR \-\-emit " json"
Emit machine-readable information to stdout. Currently supported: \fBjson\fR.
The JSON includes the selected UI mode, bind/port, URL, input source, and selected style scheme.

.TP
.BR \-\-keep-alive
In Chromium mode, do not auto-exit when Chromium closes (debugging).

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CYVIEW_STYLES
Default style scheme name to use when \fB--styles\fR is not provided.
For example:
.nf
export CYVIEW_STYLES=print
.fi

.SH STYLES CONFIGURATION
\fBcyview\fR constructs its Cytoscape.js style array dynamically.

The style collection starts with built-in defaults, then applies zero or more user-provided style files.

.TP
.B Style scheme file naming
The selected scheme determines the styles filename searched in each config directory:
.IP \(bu 2
Scheme \fBdefault\fR uses \fBcyview.styles\fR
.IP \(bu 2
Any other scheme name \fBNAME\fR uses \fBcyview.styles.NAME\fR

Examples:
.nf
cyview --styles print graph.cyjs
# searches cyview.styles.print files

CYVIEW_STYLES=dark cyview graph.cyjs
# searches cyview.styles.dark files
.fi

.TP
.B Styles file format
Each styles file contains JSON defining Cytoscape.js selector blocks.
Accepted formats are:
.IP \(bu 2
A JSON array of selector blocks
.IP \(bu 2
A JSON object containing \fBstyle\fR or \fBstyles\fR with an array value

Example (\fIcyview.styles.print\fR):
.nf
[
  {
    "selector": "node",
    "style": {
      "font-size": 14,
      "text-max-width": 240
    }
  },
  {
    "selector": "edge",
    "style": {
      "width": 3
    }
  }
]
.fi

.TP
.B Replace semantics
If the first line (leading whitespace permitted) is:
.nf
# replace
.fi
then the running style collection is cleared before applying the JSON that follows.
If the file contains only \fB# replace\fR and no JSON after it, the resulting style collection is empty (no styles).

.TP
.B Merge semantics
Styles are appended in order. Later selector blocks override earlier ones when selectors overlap (Cytoscape.js resolves conflicts by applying later styles).

.TP
.B Search order
For the selected scheme filename, style files are searched in this order:
.IP 1. 3
\fB/etc/cyview/\fR
.IP 2. 3
\fB~/.config/cyview/\fR
.IP 3. 3
The directory containing the input \fI.cyjs\fR file (only when input is a file)
.IP 4. 3
The current working directory (PWD)

.SH EXAMPLES
View a file:
.nf
cyview architecture.cyjs
.fi

Use a named scheme:
.nf
cyview --styles print architecture.cyjs
.fi

Set a default scheme via environment:
.nf
export CYVIEW_STYLES=print
cyview architecture.cyjs
.fi

Do not open a browser; just print the URL:
.nf
cyview --ui print-url architecture.cyjs
.fi

Exit after the first successful page load:
.nf
cyview --once architecture.cyjs
.fi

.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/cyview/
System-wide style overrides directory.
.TP
.B ~/.config/cyview/
User style overrides directory.
.TP
.B cyview.styles
Default scheme style file name.
.TP
.B cyview.styles.NAME
Named scheme style file name.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error or input/parse/bind error.
.TP
.B 3
Requested UI mode is unavailable (e.g., \fB--ui chromium\fR without Chromium or without a GUI).

.SH NOTES
\fBxdg-open\fR does not provide a reliable way to detect when the user closes the browser window.
Chromium app mode is used when available because it provides a dedicated window and allows \fBcyview\fR to exit when that window closes.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xdg-open (1),
.BR chromium (1)
